Thoron: the native ad-blocking engine component (components/thoron_adblock).

A small cxx bridge to adblock-rust (build an engine from filter list text, load a saved engine, give it
its resources, decide whether a request is blocked or answered with a stand-in, say what a document
needs, count a list's rules, find a rule that would allow every request) with native Rust unit tests;
build steps that take the filter lists from the pinned uBlock Origin CRX (each marked with its list id),
compile them into a saved engine (compile_engine, which checks that the result loads again) and take
uBlock Origin's scriptlets and redirect resources from the same CRX (extract_resources.py runs
assemble_resources.mjs with the build's Node.js; they stay GPL-3.0 and ship as their own data file;
scriptlets uBlock Origin marks as needing trust can only be used by uBlock Origin's own lists; a hide
selector that could add other CSS to a page is left out); an engine service that loads the updated or the saved engine on a background
thread and falls back to building from the lists; list updates (the lists uBlock Origin uses, over HTTPS
without cookies, at most every 4 days; a list that is too large, not a list, shrinks by more than half
or allows every request keeps its previous copy, and a new engine must still block every probe request
the current one blocks; the check is put on record before new lists are read, so a list that made the
parser abort could not take the browser down again at every start); a proxying URLLoaderFactory that
ends blocked subresource requests and the documents of frames inside a page with ERR_BLOCKED_BY_CLIENT,
or answers them with a harmless stand-in when a filter asks for one ($redirect, redirect-rule); and the
ThoronNativeAdblock feature (off by default).

Fuzzing (P5.17): fuzz_lists is a build-machine tool, not shipped. It feeds changed copies of real list
lines to the engine and to the code around it (engine_core.rs, cosmetics.rs, which the bridge in
engine.rs only wraps) and asks everything the browser asks, with the invariants a style sheet and a
rule for the page-side runner must keep. A Rust panic ends the browser, so reading a list has to
survive any text.

Worker scripts (P5.27): the loaders for the script a worker or shared worker starts from, and for the
scripts a service worker starts from and imports, get the request proxy too.

WebSockets (P5.29): a WebSocket connection is not a URL load. websocket.cc answers the embedder's
question about each connection of a frame: one the lists block (request type "websocket") is not
opened; any other is opened as the browser would open it.

Redirects (P5.25): a request the lists allow can be redirected to something they block, or to an
address with a parameter they take out. Every request that goes out gets a RedirectWatcher between its
loader and its client, on a background sequence (the UI thread is not put in the path of every
response), which puts each redirect target to the engine: blocked ends the request, parameters to take
out make the client and the network follow the redirect to the clean address. Documents (a page, a
frame) are handled by DocumentRequestInterceptor at the start of a navigation and after every redirect:
an internal redirect to the clean address, and for a frame ERR_BLOCKED_BY_CLIENT or the stand-in. A site
that sends the browser back to the address just cleaned is loaded as it is.

The rest of the filter syntax (P5.22):
- Procedural hide rules. adblock-rust is built without its CSS parser, so procedural.rs splits a rule's
  selector into plain CSS and tasks (has-text, upward, xpath, matches-css, matches-attr, matches-path,
  matches-media, min-text-length, others, watch-attr, nested has and not), and procedural.js, Thoron's own
  page-side runner, carries them out in an isolated world: it marks what a rule finds with an attribute
  whose name is random per document (the style sheet hides or restyles what carries it), or removes the
  element, an attribute or a class. `:style()` declarations that could fetch something or leave their
  rule are refused. A procedural rule without a site is not applied (it would put the runner on every
  page). `:shadow()` and `:matches-prop()` are not built.
- `$removeparam`: the engine reports the address without the parameters its rules name. query.rs adds
  the forms adblock-rust drops: no value (every parameter), the simple patterns the lists use, and
  exceptions; each kind is matched by a small engine of its own and saved with the main one (the saved
  engine has a header now: engine_core.rs). The request proxy sends other requests out with the clean
  address; a document's own request is cleaned through an internal redirect, so the address bar and
  the page see the clean address. Only the query may differ from the original.
- `$csp`: DocumentLoaderThrottle adds the lists' policy to a document's response, as the header and in
  the parsed form the navigation reads. Only the lists' own text is parsed in the browser (plain ASCII,
  at most 4096 bytes); a policy can only take permissions away from a page, and one that asks the page
  to send reports (report-uri, report-to) is refused.

